4.1 Open your S3 bucket
Open the AWS Management Console.
Search for S3 in the top search bar.
Click Amazon S3.
Open your website bucket.
If you haven't created a separate website bucket yet, create one first. Use a unique bucket name, such as:
elegance-website-demo-2026-387421310630
Keep the bucket in the region you plan to use for hosting.
4.2 Upload the website files
Inside your bucket:
Click Upload.
Under Files and folders, click Add files.
Select these three files from your Elegance-Website folder:
index.html
style.css
script.js
Click Add folder.
Select the images folder if you have product images.
Click Upload at the bottom of the page.
Wait for the upload to complete.
Click Close.
Step 5: Enable Static Website Hosting
5.1 Open Properties
Return to your S3 bucket.
Click the Properties tab.
Scroll down to the bottom.
Find the section called Static website hosting.
5.2 Enable website hosting
Click Edit.
Under Static website hosting, select Enable.
Under Hosting type, select Host a static website.
In the Index document field, enter:
index.html
In the Error document field, you can enter:
error.html
This is optional. If you have not created an error page, leave it empty.
Click Save changes.
AWS should now display a Bucket website endpoint in the Static website hosting section.
Don't open it yet—we need to configure the permissions first.
Step 6: Allow public access to the website
For the direct S3 website endpoint to work, website files must be publicly readable. This is only appropriate for a bucket containing files intended to be public.
6.1 Edit Block Public Access
Open your S3 bucket.
Click the Permissions tab.
Find Block public access (bucket settings).
Click Edit.
Uncheck Block all public access.
Review the warning.
Check the confirmation box if AWS asks you to acknowledge the change.
Click Save changes.
Keep this bucket limited to your public website files. Never upload private documents, passwords, credentials, or customer information here.
If AWS prevents this change because of an account-level or lab-level restriction, don't try to bypass it. Tell me what the error says.
6.2 Add a Bucket Policy
Stay on the Permissions tab.
Scroll down to Bucket policy.
Click Edit.
Paste the policy below.
Replace YOUR_BUCKET_NAME with your exact bucket name.
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "PublicReadWebsiteFiles",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::YOUR_BUCKET_NAME/*"
    }
  ]
}
For example, if your bucket name is elegance-website-demo-2026-387421310630, the Resource line should be:
"Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::elegance-website-demo-2026-387421310630/*"
5. Click Save changes.
If AWS says public policies are blocked, check whether Block Public Access is still enabled or whether your Learner Lab has an account-level restriction.
Step 7: Open your website
Return to your S3 bucket.
Click Properties.
Scroll to Static website hosting.
Copy the Bucket website endpoint.
Open it in a new browser tab.
It will look similar to:
http://your-bucket-name.s3-website.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com

